case U ∝q 1−wp·x−wp, with wp = 0.4. Alternative scoring
functions—CES at ρ ∈{−0.5, 0.5}, a linear additive rule,
and an LLM-based selection operator—are evaluated in Ap-
pendix B.3.3. ▶Matching. To model idiosyncratic client
factors, we add Gumbel noise to scores prior to ranking,
inducing stochastic job-side preferences. Given these rank-
ings and agents’ submitted preference orderings, jobs are
allocated via a job-proposing Gale–Shapley stable matching
procedure (Gale & Shapley, 1962) with capacity ν, which
limits the number of jobs an agent can be allocated per round.
This yields the job-optimal stable matching, modeling gig
platforms typically optimise for client satisfaction (Hor-
ton, 2010). Full matching pseudocode is in Appendix B.3.3
▶Task performance. If agent i is matched to a job J of type
k = τ(J) at round t, the environment produces a perfor-
mance score yt(J) ∈[0, 1], interpreted as normalised deliv-
