B.4. Algorithms and Pseudocode
Algorithm 1 AI-Work market loop (per round t)
1: Input: Latent skills {θi,k,t}i,k, reputations {Ri,k,t}i,k, job catalog J
2: Output: Updated {θi,k,t+1}i,k, {Ri,k,t+1}i,k, rewards {ri,t}i
3: Sample listings Jt ⊆J (independent Bernoulli per job); draw each posted budget bt(J).
4: Each agent outputs either (i) BID with an ordered list of up to L bids (J, pi,J,t), or (ii) TRAIN with a task id k.
5: For each job J ∈Jt and each bidding agent i, compute job-side score from Ri,τ(J),t and normalized bid xi,J,t =
pi,J,t/bt(J).
6: Apply temperature scaling and optional Gumbel perturbations to obtain stochastic job-side rankings.
7: Build job preference lists by sorting bidders by perturbed score (truncate to M); build agent preference lists from agent
bid ordering (truncate to L).
8: Run job-proposing multi-match Gale–Shapley with capacity ν to obtain a matching µt (Alg. 2).
9: For each matched job J, sample/compute performance yt(J) ∈[0, 1] from the task model using current skill or task
memory.
10: Compute payments according to the active contract condition.
11: Update skills using the task-specific learning rule: explicit training triggers deterministically; on-the-job learning
triggers with probability ϕ; unsuccessful bidding triggers fallback training on the top-priority attempted task in the
baseline setting.
12: Update reputations using both paid job outcomes and benchmark scores produced after any training event (Alg. 3).
