3.1 Households
Households are the consumer-workers in the economic system that provide labor
for production at firms, while also consuming some of the produced goods. When
employed, they are paid wages for their labor at their employer firm, and pay for the
price of consumed goods. The government collects income taxes on their labor income,
part of which could be redistributed back to households as tax credits in the sub-
sequent time step. They also earn (accrue) interest on their savings (debt) from the
central bank. These monetary inflows and outflows govern the dynamics of household
savings/deposits from one time step to the next.
