6.3.1 Utility for Monetary Policy Design
In this macroeconomic scenario, we play out the learned policies in 100 test episodes,
with a focus on central bank decision-making. The economy consists of homogeneous
households with skills ωij ≡1, heterogeneous firms with production elasticities linearly
spaced in the range αj ∈[0.05, 1.00], and low exogenous shocks σj ≡0.01. Subse-
quently, to establish a baseline for comparison and highlight the benefits of learning a
central bank policy, we substitute the learned central bank policy with a non-inertial
Taylor rule for setting interest rates, as described in [86]:
